  labText('Capstone: a reliable order platform', 'One architecture covering all four exam domains', 'Capstone', 'Submit a design and pseudocode; deploying the full system is not required, so no AWS resource charges are incurred. If you choose to deploy it, estimate costs separately and arrange complete cleanup.', 'Independently design security, scaling, retries, monitoring, and cost considerations.', [
    step('Define acceptance criteria', 'Assume a static frontend, signed-in users creating orders, sudden peaks, asynchronous notifications, no duplicate charges, and required backups. State assumptions for latency, consistency, RTO, and RPO.', 'Write at least six verifiable requirements, using values or ranges rather than vague claims such as high performance.'),
    step('Draw request and event paths', 'Design a CloudFront/S3 frontend, an API Gateway/Lambda entrance, a suitable order database, and SQS for asynchronous work. Mark where SNS fan-out is needed.', 'Clearly distinguish synchronous responses from asynchronous processing.'),
    step('Design security boundaries', 'List user authentication, least-privilege roles, private object access, TLS, key/secret management, and auditing points. Never embed credentials in the frontend.', 'Every data path has an identity or access-boundary explanation.'),
    step('Define duplicate and failure handling', 'Write idempotent createOrder(orderId) pseudocode. Design consumer retries, visibility timeouts, a DLQ, replay, and alarms.', 'Replaying the same business request does not create a second order.'),
    step('Address failures and cost', 'Explain behavior when downstream services slow down, a component fails, data is deleted, or traffic doubles. List the three main cost drivers and optimization options.', 'Optimizations preserve the stated reliability and consistency requirements.'),
    step('Review your own architecture', 'Ask two questions for each domain: security, resilience, performance, and cost. Revise the design and explain its trade-offs to an imaginary colleague in five minutes.', 'The submission includes a diagram, decisions, failure paths, and unverified assumptions.')
  ], ['Confirm no cloud resources were accidentally deployed. If you deployed extras, remove them in dependency order and check billing.', 'Do not include real secrets or user data in the documentation.'], 'A presentable architecture package: diagram, decision record, idempotency pseudocode, monitoring plan, and cost table.'),
  labText('Architecture defense and final resource audit', 'Finish by explaining clearly and cleaning thoroughly', 'Capstone', 'Use local explanations and read-only account checks. Do not restart billable resources just to prove a concept.', 'Demonstrate architectural trade-offs and confirm that lab resources are cleaned up.', [
    step('Explain six comparisons without notes', 'Spend two minutes each on SQS/SNS, ALB/NLB, Multi-AZ/read replicas, S3/EFS/EBS, CloudFront/Global Accelerator, and KMS/Secrets Manager.', 'For each group, give use cases, limitations, and one easily misread scenario.'),
    step('Sample three failure paths', 'Choose three capstone failure cases from instance/Region failure, duplicate messages, and accidental deletion. Explain detection, recovery, and remaining risks aloud.', 'Distinguish availability, backups, and disaster recovery.'),
    step('Review the entire resource inventory', 'Check every used Region for lab compute, databases, NAT, endpoints, load balancers, object versions, log groups, queues, and dedicated IAM resources. Handle only resources clearly belonging to these labs.', 'Every item is marked deleted, deliberately retained with a reason, or pending action.'),
    step('Check spending and knowledge gaps', 'Review spending trends, remembering billing delays. Add concepts you still cannot explain to the review list. A zero balance today does not prove every resource was removed.', 'Set a date for the next billing check and list outstanding items.'),
    step('Decide whether to book', 'Use two timed mocks, domain-level results, unseen official practice, and lab completion together. If targets are unmet, plan 1–2 weeks of focused study; if met, check the official exam requirements again.', 'Record the evidence behind the decision instead of relying only on finishing eight weeks.')
  ], ['Verify cleanup of every lab resource item by item.', 'Keep useful study notes and budget notifications; remove temporary download links and credential traces.'], 'A readiness checklist, records of six spoken service comparisons, and the final cleanup inventory.')
